{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790300840,"nonce":"mTY5tRmXPSVb8e2JBq4xW/OZsew=","title":"Lua: code that load compiles cannot use debug on the main program's closures","message":"A program keeps its call stack, upvalue accessors and rebindable typing only when its own source reaches the debug library (the global, require \"debug\", _G/_ENV/package.loaded). Code that load, loadfile or dofile compiles later is not seen when the main chunk is lowered, so a loaded chunk that calls debug on the main program's functions gets nothing back and cannot rebind them.\n\nReproducer:\n\n    local counter = 0\n    local function inc() counter = counter + 1 return counter end\n    print(inc(), inc())\n    local chunk = load(\"local f = ...; print(debug.getupvalue(f, 1)); print(debug.setupvalue(f, 1, 100)); print(debug.traceback('in load'))\")\n    chunk(inc)\n    print(inc())\n\nlua5.4 prints `counter 2`, `counter`, a traceback through `local 'chunk'` and the main chunk, then 101. zylua prints empty getupvalue/setupvalue results, a traceback with only the loaded chunk, then 3.\n\nMaking every program that calls load keep frames would cost load-heavy programs that never use debug. A sound fix needs the main chunk's metadata registered when dynamic code is present (cheap, once) plus a way to invalidate typed upvalues when loaded code rebinds them (recompile or deoptimise the affected functions).","files":null}]}